\title{Degree Ceilings for Feature Encodings:\\
An Exact Finite-Sample Theory of Why Singleton Phase Features\\
Cannot Detect Alignment}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop an exact, finite-sample algebraic theory of incremental $R^2$ for feature encodings on product sample spaces, and use it to prove that an entire family of encodings---those built from arbitrary functions of a single coordinate---is provably blind to \emph{alignment targets}, while an explicitly exhibited degree-two encoding predicts the same targets perfectly.

The theory rests on a single exact identity, the bias/variance/alignment split of the mean squared error of an arbitrary predictor. From it we derive that a predictor with zero empirical covariance with the target can never beat the intercept-only baseline and is strictly worse unless constant, with excess error equal exactly to its own variance plus its squared mean offset; and that the optimal one-feature affine model attains $R^2$ equal exactly to the squared empirical correlation, with the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality for the empirical covariance obtained as a by-product of the regression identity.

On a product $A \times B$ we prove that the alignment indicator $g_\sigma(a,b) = \ind{b = \sigma(a)}$ of a bijection $\sigma$ has covariance identically zero with every additive predictor $u(a) + v(b)$, for arbitrary $u,v$; that this persists after adjoining arbitrarily many features from an independent nuisance block; and yet that $g_\sigma$ is exactly a sum of $|A|$ products of pairs of one-hot singleton features, hence attains $R^2 = 1$ under a degree-two encoding. The general mechanism is an exact orthogonal analysis-of-variance split $\Var f = \Var \fadd + \Var \fint$ valid for every target on a product grid, together with the identification of the supremum of $R^2$ over all singleton encodings as the computable ratio $\Var \fadd / \Var f$: a \emph{degree-one ceiling} available before any model is fit. For alignment targets the additive part is constant, so the ceiling is $0$ and the unreachable excess is $100\%$.

Relabelling invariance shows that every quantity of the calculus is preserved by bijections of the sample space, so an exact null is automatically window-stable with cross-window to same-window ratio $1$; observed stability is therefore not evidence of degree-one signal. Finally, the degree hierarchy does not terminate at two: on $G^3$ for a finite abelian group $G$, the zero-sum target $\ind{a+b+c=0}$ has covariance exactly zero with every predictor assembled from arbitrary functions of pairs of coordinates, while the degree-three encoding is perfect.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The empirical situation}

A model is fit to a finite dataset. To an existing baseline one adds a family of cheap, interpretable \emph{phase} features: for each observation, a distinguished landmark position is reduced modulo a prime $p$, and the resulting residue is encoded---as a one-hot dummy, as $\cos(2\pi k/p)$ and $\sin(2\pi k /p)$, or in any other per-observation way. The construction is repeated across a range of primes.

In the programme that motivated this work, that range was $3 \le p \le 97$, split into a low band and a high band. The low band produced negative transfer. The high band produced a same-window improvement in $R^2$ of $+0.0215$, with confidence interval $[-0.0025, +0.0429]$---an interval excluding the pre-registered threshold of $+0.05$ and containing $0$---but, unlike the low band, this small gain was window-stable, with a cross-window to same-window ratio of $0.92$.

Such a result is genuinely ambiguous under a purely statistical reading: small and not significant, yet stable, and stability is normally read as evidence of a real effect. We ask whether the ambiguity can be removed structurally rather than by more data. It can: the relevant class of encodings is identically uncorrelated with the relevant class of targets---not approximately, but exactly, for every modulus and every choice of per-coordinate function.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item An exact finite-sample $R^2$ calculus (Section~\ref{sec:calculus}): no asymptotics, no distributional assumptions, only averages over a finite index set.
\item A closure theorem for singleton encodings against alignment targets (Section~\ref{sec:alignment}), its nuisance-block-invariant form, and an explicit degree-two encoding achieving $R^2 = 1$ on the same target.
\item A general exact orthogonal split of any target on a product grid into degree-one and pure-interaction parts, with the supremum of achievable degree-one $R^2$ identified explicitly (Section~\ref{sec:anova}).
\item Relabelling invariance, hence automatic window stability of exact nulls (Section~\ref{sec:reindex}).
\item A degree hierarchy: three-way alignment is orthogonal to the entire pairwise layer (Section~\ref{sec:triple}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Setting and notation}

Throughout, $I$ is a finite nonempty index set, the sample. Predictors and targets are real functions on $I$.

\begin{definition}[Empirical mean, covariance, variance]
For $f,g : I \to \R$,
\[
\avg f = \frac{1}{|I|}\sum_{i \in I} f(i), \qquad
\Cov(f,g) = \avg(fg) - (\avg f)(\avg g), \qquad
\Var f = \Cov(f,f).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Mean squared error and $R^2$]
For a target $y$ and predictor $h$,
\[
\MSE(y,h) = \avg\big((y-h)^2\big), \qquad
R^2(y,h) = 1 - \frac{\MSE(y,h)}{\Var y} \quad (\Var y \ne 0).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Elementary properties]\label{lem:elem}
$\avg$ is linear and fixes constants; $\Var f \ge 0$; $\Var f = 0$ iff $f$ is constant; $\Cov(y,c) = 0$ for constant $c$; and $\Cov$ is symmetric and additive in each argument.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Linearity is a rearrangement of finite sums. Expanding gives $\Var f = \avg\big((f - \avg f)^2\big)$, whence nonnegativity; a nonnegative finite average vanishes only if every term vanishes, which gives the equality case.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Baseline calibration]
$\MSE(y, \avg y) = \Var y$, so the constant predictor has $R^2 = 0$ whenever $\Var y \ne 0$. Gain always means gain over the constant model.
\end{lemma}

\section{The exact incremental-$R^2$ calculus}\label{sec:calculus}

\begin{theorem}[Exact bias/variance/alignment split]\label{thm:decomp}
For every target $y$ and every predictor $h$,
\[
\MSE(y,h) = \Var y - 2\Cov(y,h) + \Var h + \big(\avg y - \avg h\big)^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $(y-h)^2$ and average: $\MSE(y,h) = \avg(y^2) - 2\avg(yh) + \avg(h^2)$. Substitute $\avg(y^2) = \Var y + (\avg y)^2$, $\avg(h^2) = \Var h + (\avg h)^2$, $\avg(yh) = \Cov(y,h) + (\avg y)(\avg h)$, and collect the pure-mean terms into a square.
\end{proof}

The only channel by which a predictor reduces error is the covariance term; every other term is a nonnegative penalty.

\begin{theorem}[No gain, exact excess, strict harm]\label{thm:nogain}
Suppose $\Cov(y,h) = 0$. Then $\Var y \le \MSE(y,h)$, and
\[
\MSE(y,h) - \Var y = \Var h + \big(\avg y - \avg h\big)^2 .
\]
If moreover $\Var h \neq 0$ then $\Var y < \MSE(y,h)$ strictly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:nonpos}
If $\Cov(y,h) = 0$ and $\Var y > 0$ then $R^2(y,h) \le 0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Set the covariance term of Theorem~\ref{thm:decomp} to zero; the remaining terms are a variance and a square. Divide by $\Var y > 0$ for the corollary.
\end{proof}

An uncorrelated feature is thus not neutral: fitted without regularisation it injects its own variance, and that variance is exactly the excess error.

\begin{theorem}[Optimal affine fit]\label{thm:affine}
Let $\Var f > 0$. For all $a,b \in \R$,
\[
\MSE(y, af+b) \;\ge\; \Var y - \frac{\Cov(y,f)^2}{\Var f},
\]
with equality at $a^\star = \Cov(y,f)/\Var f$, $b^\star = \avg y - a^\star \avg f$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:decomp} with $\Cov(y, af+b) = a\Cov(y,f)$, $\Var(af+b) = a^2 \Var f$, $\avg(af+b) = a \avg f + b$, and complete the square: the error equals
\[
\Var y - \frac{\Cov(y,f)^2}{\Var f} + \frac{\big(a\Var f - \Cov(y,f)\big)^2}{\Var f} + \big(\avg y - a\avg f - b\big)^2 .
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cauchy--Schwarz for the empirical covariance]
$\Cov(y,f)^2 \le \Var y \cdot \Var f$ for all $y,f$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\Var f = 0$ then $f$ is constant and $\Cov(y,f)=0$. Otherwise the optimal error of Theorem~\ref{thm:affine} is a mean of squares, hence nonnegative.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Best one-feature $R^2$ is the squared correlation]\label{thm:corr}
If $\Var f > 0$ and $\Var y > 0$ then for all $a,b$,
\[
R^2(y, af+b) \le \frac{\Cov(y,f)^2}{\Var y \, \Var f},
\]
with equality at $(a^\star,b^\star)$. Moreover $R^2(y,y) = 1$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Alignment targets and singleton encodings}\label{sec:alignment}

Fix finite nonempty sets $A,B$ and a bijection $\sigma : A \to B$; write $m = |A| = |B|$. The sample space is the grid $A \times B$.

\begin{definition}[Alignment indicator]
$g_\sigma : A \times B \to \R$, \quad $g_\sigma(a,b) = \ind{b = \sigma(a)}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Singleton, or additive, encoding]
For $u : A \to \R$ and $v : B \to \R$, put $(u \oplus v)(a,b) = u(a) + v(b)$.
\end{definition}

The functions $u, v$ are arbitrary: one-hot dummies, sinusoids of a residue, learned per-coordinate embeddings and monotone transforms are all special cases. The family is closed under sums and scalar multiples, so a linear model on \emph{any number} of per-coordinate features, with optimally fitted coefficients, is again a single member; and it contains the constant predictor, so all statements below are about incremental gain.

\begin{lemma}[Statistics of the target]\label{lem:gstat}
$\avg g_\sigma = 1/m$ and $\Var g_\sigma = 1/m - 1/m^2$, strictly positive for $m \ge 2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$g_\sigma$ equals $1$ on exactly the $m$ cells of the graph of $\sigma$ among $m^2$ cells. Being a $0/1$ indicator, $g_\sigma^2 = g_\sigma$, so $\Var g_\sigma = \avg g_\sigma - (\avg g_\sigma)^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Product structure]\label{lem:prod}
If $\phi$ depends only on the first coordinate and $\psi$ only on the second, then their grid averages agree with their factor averages and $\Cov(\phi,\psi) = 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\sum_{a,b}\phi(a)\psi(b) = \big(\sum_a \phi(a)\big)\big(\sum_b\psi(b)\big)$; divide by $|A||B|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The singleton route is closed, identically]\label{thm:closed}
For every bijection $\sigma$ and all $u : A \to \R$, $v : B \to \R$,
\[
\Cov\big(g_\sigma, \, u \oplus v\big) = 0 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $g_\sigma$ is supported on the graph of $\sigma$,
\[
\sum_{(a,b)} g_\sigma(a,b)\big(u(a)+v(b)\big) = \sum_{a\in A}\big(u(a) + v(\sigma a)\big) = \sum_a u(a) + \sum_b v(b),
\]
using bijectivity of $\sigma$ in the last step; dividing by $m^2$ gives $\avg\big(g_\sigma (u\oplus v)\big)$. On the other hand $\avg g_\sigma \cdot \avg(u \oplus v) = \frac1m\big(\frac{\sum_a u(a)}{m} + \frac{\sum_b v(b)}{m}\big)$, the same number. The difference is $0$.
\end{proof}

The graph of a bijection meets each row and each column exactly once, so restricting a marginal function to the graph and averaging returns its unrestricted average: from the point of view of any single coordinate, an alignment target is indistinguishable from a constant.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:aligngain}
For all $\sigma,u,v$: $\Var g_\sigma \le \MSE(g_\sigma, u\oplus v)$, strictly if $u \oplus v$ is nonconstant; the excess is exactly $\Var(u\oplus v) + (\avg g_\sigma - \avg(u\oplus v))^2$; and if $m \ge 2$ then $R^2(g_\sigma, u \oplus v) \le 0$.
\end{corollary}

In particular the attainable population gain is never $+0.05$, and never even $+0.0215$. Taking $v \equiv 0$ shows that any single-coordinate feature has covariance $0$ with $g_\sigma$, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:corr} its best affine model has $R^2 = 0$ exactly.

\begin{theorem}[Nuisance-block invariance]\label{thm:nuisance}
Let $C$ be finite nonempty, and on $(A\times B)\times C$ let $\tilde g(x,c) = g_\sigma(x)$. Then for all $u,v$ and every $w : C \to \R$,
\[
\Cov\Big(\tilde g,\; (x,c)\mapsto (u\oplus v)(x) + w(c)\Big) = 0,
\]
hence $\Var \tilde g \le \MSE\big(\tilde g, (u\oplus v)+w\big)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Covariance is additive in the second argument. The $(u\oplus v)$ part reduces to Theorem~\ref{thm:closed} because covariances of functions of the aligned block are computed inside that block; the $w$ part vanishes by Lemma~\ref{lem:prod}.
\end{proof}

Since $w$ is arbitrary, this covers any collection of features from other primes, windows or channels, fitted jointly, provided they do not couple to the aligned pair.

\begin{definition}[One-hot singleton features]
For $c \in A$, $d \in B$, let $e_c(a,b) = \ind{a=c}$ and $\varepsilon_d(a,b) = \ind{b=d}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact degree-two structure]\label{thm:deg2}
For all $(a,b) \in A\times B$,
\[
g_\sigma(a,b) = \sum_{c\in A} e_c(a,b)\,\varepsilon_{\sigma(c)}(a,b).
\]
Consequently the predictor $h_\star = \sum_{c} e_c \varepsilon_{\sigma(c)}$ has $\MSE(g_\sigma, h_\star) = 0$ and $R^2(g_\sigma,h_\star)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The $c$-th summand is $1$ exactly when $a=c$ and $b=\sigma(c)$; at most one summand is nonzero, and one is nonzero exactly when $b = \sigma(a)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Separation]\label{thm:sep}
Let $m \ge 2$. Then $\sup_{u,v} R^2(g_\sigma, u\oplus v) \le 0$ while $R^2(g_\sigma, h_\star) = 1$.
\end{theorem}

Specialising to $A = B = \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$ and $\sigma = \mathrm{id}$ gives the diagonal alignment target $d_p(a,b) = \ind{a=b}$.

\begin{corollary}[The prime windows]\label{cor:primes}
For every $p \ge 2$: $\Var d_p = 1/p - 1/p^2 > 0$, every singleton encoding has $R^2(d_p, u\oplus v)\le 0$, and the degree-two one-hot interaction encoding attains $R^2 = 1$. This holds in particular at $p = 3$ and $p = 97$ and uniformly on $3 \le p \le 97$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Where the excess lives: exact degree-one/degree-two accounting}\label{sec:anova}

Let $A,B$ be finite nonempty and $f : A\times B \to \R$ arbitrary.

\begin{definition}[Marginal means and the two parts]
Let $r_f(a) = \avg_{b} f(a,b)$, $c_f(b) = \avg_a f(a,b)$, $\mu_f = \avg f$, and
\[
\fadd(a,b) = r_f(a) + c_f(b) - \mu_f, \qquad \fint = f - \fadd .
\]
By construction $f = \fadd + \fint$ and $\fadd$ is additive with $u = r_f$, $v = c_f - \mu_f$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Zero marginals]\label{lem:zeromarg}
$\avg \fadd = \mu_f$, $\avg \fint = 0$, and for every $a$ and every $b$,
$\sum_{b'} \fint(a,b') = 0$ and $\sum_{a'} \fint(a',b) = 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both marginal means average to the grand mean, giving $\avg \fadd = \mu_f$. Fixing $a$ and averaging over $b$ gives $r_f(a)$ for both $f$ and $\fadd$; symmetrically for columns.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Orthogonality of the interaction part]\label{thm:orth}
$\Cov(\fint, u\oplus v) = 0$ for all $u,v$; in particular $\Cov(\fadd,\fint) = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
As $\avg \fint = 0$, the covariance is $\avg(\fint\cdot(u\oplus v))$; and $\sum_{a,b}\fint(a,b)u(a) = \sum_a u(a)\sum_b \fint(a,b) = 0$ by the row identity, symmetrically for $v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact variance budget]\label{thm:budget}
$\Var f = \Var \fadd + \Var \fint$, with no cross term.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Best additive predictor]\label{thm:bestadd}
For all $u,v$, $\MSE(f, u\oplus v) \ge \Var \fint$, with equality for $u \oplus v = \fadd$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $f - (u\oplus v) = \fint + \big(\fadd - (u\oplus v)\big)$ with the bracket additive. Expanding the mean square, the cross term is $\avg(\fint \cdot \text{additive}) = 0$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:zeromarg}, and the remaining terms are $\avg(\fint^2) = \Var \fint$ plus a nonnegative mean square that vanishes exactly at $\fadd$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Degree-one ceiling]\label{thm:ceiling}
If $\Var f > 0$ then for all $u,v$,
\[
R^2(f, u\oplus v) \le \frac{\Var \fadd}{\Var f},
\]
attained at $u\oplus v = \fadd$. Equivalently $\sup_{u,v} R^2(f,u\oplus v) = \Var \fadd / \Var f$, and the unreachable excess is $\Var \fint / \Var f$.
\end{theorem}

The ceiling depends on the target and the grid only---not on which features one has thought of, nor on the estimator---and is computed in two passes over the data.

\begin{theorem}[Alignment targets have ceiling zero]\label{thm:ceilzero}
For $g_\sigma$ on $A\times B$ with $m \ge 2$, every row mean and every column mean equals $1/m$, so $(g_\sigma)_{\mathrm{add}} \equiv 1/m$ is constant, $\Var (g_\sigma)_{\mathrm{add}} = 0$, the ceiling is $0$, and $\Var (g_\sigma)_{\mathrm{int}} = \Var g_\sigma$.
\end{theorem}

Thus Theorem~\ref{thm:closed} is the extreme case of a general accounting identity: alignment targets are $100\%$ interaction.

\section{Relabelling invariance and window stability}\label{sec:reindex}

\begin{theorem}[Invariance under relabelling]\label{thm:reindex}
Let $e : J \to I$ be a bijection of finite nonempty index sets. Then for all $y,h : I \to \R$,
\[
\avg(y\circ e) = \avg y,\quad \Cov(y\circ e, h\circ e) = \Cov(y,h),\quad \Var(y\circ e) = \Var y,
\]
\[
\MSE(y\circ e, h\circ e) = \MSE(y,h), \qquad R^2(y\circ e, h\circ e) = R^2(y,h).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Reindexing a finite sum along a bijection preserves it, and $|J| = |I|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Stability of a null]\label{cor:stab}
An exact population null transports to a relabelled window as an exact null, so its cross-window to same-window ratio is exactly $1$---as for a genuine nonzero effect.
\end{corollary}

Stability therefore does not discriminate between a small true degree-one effect and an exact null. What discriminates is the level, and Corollary~\ref{cor:primes} fixes the level at $\le 0$: a stable $+0.02$ against a proven ceiling of $0$ describes estimator behaviour, not signal.

\section{The degree hierarchy does not stop at two}\label{sec:triple}

Let $G$ be a finite abelian group with $N = |G| \ge 2$, canonically $G = \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$, and work on $G\times G\times G$.

\begin{definition}[Three-way alignment and pairwise encodings]
$t(a,b,c) = \ind{a+b+c=0}$; and for arbitrary $F,H,K : G\times G \to \R$,
\[
P_{F,H,K}(a,b,c) = F(a,b) + H(b,c) + K(a,c).
\]
The family $P$ contains all degree-one encodings and the entire interaction layer that solves the two-coordinate problem.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$\avg t = 1/N$ and $\Var t = 1/N - 1/N^2 > 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For each of the $N^2$ pairs $(a,b)$ exactly one $c = -(a+b)$ gives $t=1$; the indicator is idempotent.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The pairwise layer is blind]\label{thm:tri}
$\Cov\big(t, P_{F,H,K}\big) = 0$ for all $F,H,K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Treat the three terms separately. For $F$: summing $t(a,b,c)F(a,b)$, for each pair $(a,b)$ exactly one $c$ contributes, so the sum is $\sum_{a,b}F(a,b)$ and $\avg(tF) = \frac1N \avg F = \avg t \cdot \avg F$, contributing zero covariance. The other two terms are identical after permuting coordinates.
\end{proof}

Conceptually, conditioning on any two of the three coordinates leaves the conditional mean of $t$ equal to the constant $1/N$; a function omitting a coordinate cannot detect a target whose conditional mean is flat in that direction.

\begin{theorem}[Degree-three separation]
For $N \ge 2$, every pairwise encoding has $R^2(t, P_{F,H,K}) \le 0$, while $R^2(t,t) = 1$. In particular this holds at $N = 97$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{conjecture}[Degree-$k$ blindness]\label{conj:k}
On $G^k$, the zero-sum indicator $\ind{a_1 + \cdots + a_k = 0}$ has covariance exactly $0$ with every function omitting at least one coordinate; hence every encoding of degree $< k$ attains population $R^2 \le 0$, while the degree-$k$ encoding attains $R^2 = 1$. The cases $k = 2$ and $k = 3$ are Theorems~\ref{thm:closed} and~\ref{thm:tri}, proved by the same template.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Degree-one ceiling.} Given $f$ tabulated on an $|A|\times|B|$ grid: compute the grand mean, row means and column means in one pass; accumulate $\Var f$ and $\Var \fadd$ in a second; return $\Var\fadd/\Var f$. Cost $O(|A||B|)$ time and $O(|A|+|B|)$ extra space. By Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling} the output is exact and attained.

\paragraph{Certified futility check.} Given $f$ and a candidate $h$: compute $\Cov(f,h)$, $\Var h$, $\avg f$, $\avg h$ in $O(|I|)$. If the covariance vanishes, report the exact excess error $\Var h + (\avg f - \avg h)^2$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:nogain}, a certificate of nonpositive---indeed strictly negative for nonconstant $h$---$R^2$. Otherwise report the exact optimal single-feature $R^2 = \Cov(f,h)^2/(\Var f \Var h)$.

\paragraph{Degree ladder scan.} Project the target orthogonally onto functions of at most $d$ coordinates and report the projected variance fraction, for $d = 1,\dots,D$. The ladder is monotone in $d$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:tri} it can be flat at $0$ up to $d = k-1$ and jump to $1$ at $d = k$, so the location of the jump identifies the interaction order.

\section{Discussion}

The motivating question is settled in the negative for the stated class of encodings, and settled by an identity rather than a test. Three escape routes are closed: richer per-coordinate functions (by the arbitrariness of $u,v$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:closed} and closure of the family under sums); features from other primes or windows added alongside (Theorem~\ref{thm:nuisance}); and the appeal to window stability as corroboration (Corollary~\ref{cor:stab}).

The theory does not assert that a residual excess in any given dataset \emph{is} a joint alignment. If the true target is only partly relational, the statement becomes quantitative and Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling} supplies the exact number. A useful tension to record: at the population level an uncorrelated feature has $R^2 \le 0$, yet finite-sample training $R^2$ is typically positive, because the fitted coefficient absorbs sampling covariance; out of sample it reverts. A small measured gain against a proven zero ceiling is therefore evidence of incomplete reversion, for instance because evaluation windows share structure with the fitting window.

Practically: compute the ceiling before engineering the features. Two passes answer, exactly, a question otherwise attacked by an unbounded sequence of experiments. When the ceiling is zero, the productive response is to raise the degree, not the sample size.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Degree-$k$ blindness.} Prove Conjecture~\ref{conj:k} in general: conditioning on any $k-1$ coordinates leaves the conditional mean at $1/N$. This is the sharp prediction to test against the next encoding round before committing compute.
\item \textbf{Ceiling arithmetic for composite moduli.} $\mathbb{Z}/pq\mathbb{Z} \cong \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}/q\mathbb{Z}$ turns a single-modulus alignment into a two-block alignment; the resulting arithmetic should predict how gains compose across coprime moduli.
\item \textbf{Partial alignment.} Replace the graph of a bijection by a relation of controlled density, and track how the ceiling grows away from zero, giving a calibration curve for small measured gains.
\item \textbf{Estimator-side theory.} Predict the finite-sample distribution of measured incremental $R^2$ under a proven zero ceiling.
\item \textbf{Beyond squared error.} Extend the exact accounting to other proper losses; the orthogonality arguments are Hilbert-space arguments on a finite grid, and Bregman analogues would broaden the reach of the ceiling diagnostic.
\end{itemize}

